.TH PW\-CANDIDATES 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.3.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
pw\-candidates \- expand a token list into password candidates (btcrecover\-style, from public
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
pw\-candidates [\-\-max N] [\-\-rules case,swap,typo,digit] [FILE]: expand a token list (one token per line, or space\-separated on stdin) into password candidates. Output CONTAINS SECRETS (one candidate per line). Default rules: case,swap,digit. Default \-\-max 1000. Pipe into a wallet opener.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
docs only: product of tokens, case variants, adjacent swaps, digit append/prepend,
keyboard\-adjacent substitutions). Bounded by \-\-max. Output CONTAINS SECRETS (one
candidate per line). Pipe into a wallet opener: `pw\-candidates T | while read p; do
printf '%s\en' "$p"; cat FILE; done | opener`.
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
\-\-max
\-\-rules
.fi
Each flag's meaning is in the SYNOPSIS usage line(s) above (taken from \-\-help).
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
keyboard\-adjacent substitutions). Bounded by \-\-max. Output CONTAINS SECRETS (one
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
no status field
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ echo hunter 2 | pw\-candidates \-\-max 5
# exit 0, no status field
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
